This is a placeholder, not a considered legal decision. No license has been
chosen yet, so this file defaults to the conservative option: all rights
reserved, no permission granted to copy, modify, or distribute this code.

This default was picked deliberately over a permissive open-source license
(MIT, Apache 2.0, etc.) because Terrium's business model (see
Business/ROADMAP.md and the pitch deck's pricing tiers) assumes institutional
licensing revenue, and giving the whole codebase away under a permissive
license before that model is validated would foreclose an option rather
than preserve one. That reasoning itself should be revisited, not assumed
correct forever -- plenty of research-tooling startups do go open-core or
fully OSS deliberately, often for exactly the credibility and adoption
reasons this project cares about (see README.md's "traceable to a citation"
framing).

Decide this with a lawyer as part of actual incorporation
(see Business/INCORPORATION_CHECKLIST.md), not by editing this file
directly without that conversation happening first.
